# Intervue AI — API smoke-test sequence
#
# The 24-step end-to-end flow from master-note §66, as a runnable HTTP file
# (VS Code REST Client / JetBrains HTTP Client format).
#
# HOW TO USE
#   1. Start the backend + Postgres:  pnpm --filter backend start:dev
#   2. Set @internalToken below from services/backend/.env (INTERNAL_SERVICE_SECRET).
#   3. Run the requests top to bottom. Steps that mint an id print it in the
#      response — paste it into the matching @variable at the top before the
#      next step that needs it.
#
# A plain .http file cannot chain response values portably across clients, so
# for the fully automated, self-chaining version of this same sequence use
# `python infra/postman/run_collection.py` (56 assertions). That runner is the
# regression gate; this file is the readable specification and demo script.

@base = http://localhost:3000
@internalToken = REPLACE_WITH_INTERNAL_SERVICE_SECRET

# --- ids produced by earlier steps (paste as you go) -------------------------
@orgId =
@pipelineId =
@stage1Id =
@stage2Id =
@questionVersionId =
@jobPostingId =
@applicationId =
@inviteToken =
@interviewId =
@answerId =
@evaluationId =
@decisionId =

# A fresh email per run keeps the script re-runnable.
@email = smoke-{{$timestamp}}@intervue.test
@password = RunnerSecure!2026


### 1. Authentication — sign up
POST {{base}}/api/auth/sign-up/email
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: http://localhost:3001

{ "email": "{{email}}", "password": "{{password}}", "name": "Smoke Runner" }


### 1b. Authentication — sign in (cookies now held by the client)
POST {{base}}/api/auth/sign-in/email
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: http://localhost:3001

{ "email": "{{email}}", "password": "{{password}}" }


### 2. Create organization  → capture id as @orgId
POST {{base}}/api/auth/organization/create
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: http://localhost:3001

{ "name": "Smoke Org", "slug": "smoke-org-{{$timestamp}}" }


### 2b. Set the organization active (required before any org-scoped call)
POST {{base}}/api/auth/organization/set-active
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: http://localhost:3001

{ "organizationId": "{{orgId}}" }


### 3. Create pipeline  → @pipelineId
POST {{base}}/api/pipelines
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Backend Interview" }


### 4. Add stages  → @stage1Id, @stage2Id
POST {{base}}/api/pipelines/{{pipelineId}}/stages
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Technical", "order": 1 }

### 4b. Second stage
POST {{base}}/api/pipelines/{{pipelineId}}/stages
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Project Experience", "order": 2 }


### 5. Add questions  → @questionVersionId (first question of stage 1)
POST {{base}}/api/stages/{{stage1Id}}/questions
Content-Type: application/json

{ "question": "Explain SQL vs NoSQL databases.", "type": "MULTI_LINE", "order": 1 }

### 5b. Second question
POST {{base}}/api/stages/{{stage1Id}}/questions
Content-Type: application/json

{ "question": "How would you design a rate limiter?", "type": "MULTI_LINE", "order": 2 }


### 6. Add rubric criteria
POST {{base}}/api/stages/{{stage1Id}}/questions/{{questionVersionId}}/rubrics
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Database fundamentals", "description": "SQL vs NoSQL trade-offs", "maxScore": 5 }

### 6b. Second criterion
POST {{base}}/api/stages/{{stage1Id}}/questions/{{questionVersionId}}/rubrics
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Clarity", "maxScore": 3 }


### 7. Publish pipeline
# NOTE: there is no standalone publish-pipeline endpoint. The immutable snapshot
# (PipelineVersion → PipelineStageVersion → QuestionVersion → options/rubrics)
# is minted by step 9, when the job posting is published. Nothing after that
# point can mutate the snapshot an interview is running against.


### 8. Create job posting  → @jobPostingId   (status DRAFT)
POST {{base}}/api/job-postings
Content-Type: application/json

{ "title": "Backend Developer", "description": "Smoke job", "pipelineId": "{{pipelineId}}" }


### 9. Publish job  (mints the pipeline snapshot; sets jobPosting.pipelineVersionId)
POST {{base}}/api/job-postings/{{jobPostingId}}/publish

### 9b. Publishing twice must conflict (snapshot already frozen) — expect 400/409
POST {{base}}/api/job-postings/{{jobPostingId}}/publish


### 10/11/12. Invite candidate → creates candidate + application + invite token
POST {{base}}/api/applications/invite
Content-Type: application/json

{ "firstName": "Ada", "lastName": "Lovelace", "email": "ada+{{$timestamp}}@intervue.test", "jobPostingId": "{{jobPostingId}}" }


### 13. Create interview  → @interviewId
POST {{base}}/api/interviews
Content-Type: application/json

{ "applicationId": "{{applicationId}}" }


### 14. Access interview by invite token (candidate-facing, no account)
GET {{base}}/api/interviews/access/{{inviteToken}}


### 15. Start interview
POST {{base}}/api/interviews/{{interviewId}}/start


### 16. Submit answer against the current question  → @answerId
POST {{base}}/api/interviews/{{interviewId}}/answers
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "questionId": "{{questionVersionId}}",
  "content": "SQL databases are relational and provide ACID guarantees with complex joins, while NoSQL trades some guarantees for horizontal scale and flexible schemas."
}


### 17/18. Progress questions, then stages
GET {{base}}/api/interviews/{{interviewId}}/current
# Repeat step 16 with the returned next question. When the last question of a
# stage is answered, the backend completes the stage and moves to the next one
# inside the SAME transaction — no separate advance call exists, by design.


### 19. Complete interview (idempotent — safe to call again)
POST {{base}}/api/internal/interviews/{{interviewId}}/complete
Authorization: Internal {{internalToken}}


### 20. Trigger AI evaluation — one row per model
POST {{base}}/api/internal/evaluations
Authorization: Internal {{internalToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "interviewSessionId": "{{interviewId}}", "answerId": "{{answerId}}", "model": "openai/gpt-4o-mini" }


### 20b. Complete that evaluation
PATCH {{base}}/api/internal/evaluations/{{evaluationId}}/complete
Authorization: Internal {{internalToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "score": 7, "reasoning": "solid fundamentals", "result": { "criteria": [] } }


### 21. Persist scores (upsert is idempotent on applicationId+questionVersionId)
POST {{base}}/api/internal/scores
Authorization: Internal {{internalToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "applicationId": "{{applicationId}}",
  "questionVersionId": "{{questionVersionId}}",
  "overallScore": 7,
  "maxScore": 8,
  "reasoning": "smoke score",
  "criteria": []
}


### 22. Generate decision (AI recommendation)  → @decisionId
POST {{base}}/api/internal/applications/{{applicationId}}/decision
Authorization: Internal {{internalToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "outcome": "PASS", "totalScore": 7, "maxScore": 8, "reasoning": "threshold 87.5% vs 70%", "requestedBy": "ai-service" }


### 23. Override decision (HUMAN — requires the decision:override permission)
# Requires a signed-in user whose role holds `decision:override` (owner, admin or
# recruiter) AND whose active organization owns this application. The previous
# outcome is preserved in a DecisionOverride row — never overwritten (§44).
POST {{base}}/api/interviews/{{interviewId}}/decision/override
Content-Type: application/json

{ "newOutcome": "FAIL", "reason": "Strong production experience not captured by rubric." }

### 23b. Overriding to the SAME outcome must fail — expect 400
POST {{base}}/api/interviews/{{interviewId}}/decision/override
Content-Type: application/json

{ "newOutcome": "FAIL", "reason": "no-op" }


### 24. Verify the audit chain
# Expect DECISION_CREATED (actorType AI) and DECISION_OVERRIDDEN (actorType USER)
# — AI actions must stay distinguishable from human actions forever (§64 r3).
GET {{base}}/api/internal/audit/{{orgId}}
Authorization: Internal {{internalToken}}
